From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: knapcio <knapcio@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 08:03:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/30] Decode: paced side-stream L2 prefetch of the next
 weights in the step's idle-DRAM windows (G6_L2PF)

The decode step leaves DRAM idle in its collective waits (the two all-reduces, the
G6_QA_SPLIT / G6_IDXQ_SPLIT gathers) and through most of the latency-bound attention,
while the GEMVs around them run below the DRAM rate cold (x_in ~120 GB/s, absorb ~124,
o_proj 210). With G6_L2PF one warp on a high-priority side stream issues 8 KiB
cp.async.bulk.prefetch.L2 chunks of the layer's next weights from a token bucket
(G6_L2PF_GBPS, waves of G6_L2PF_WAVE), as blaesus/dsgx10's paced prefetch does,
instead of G6_OPROJ_PF's 8-CTA burst; a second warp polls the collective's tails and
stops the window once it has arrived. One queue per layer in read order (x_in, idx_q,
q_b, UK, UV, o_proj capped at G6_L2PF_OPMB, router, shared fc1), each window starting
at its first unread target and continuing where the layer's last window ended.
Windows (mask): 1 the MLP all-reduce wait (next layer), 2 the gathers, 4 the attention,
8 the attention all-reduce wait. Budgets G6_L2PF_US / G6_L2PF_MB per window; targets
G6_L2PF_T. The side stream is forked per window and joined once at the end of the
step (part). A prefetch writes nothing and changes no load's value: same bytes out.
Unset / 0: no stream, no launch, the graphs are unchanged.

engine/kernels/tests/l2pf_bench.cu: 78 distinct layers of the decode sequence on one
GPU in one graph (x_in tiles, q_b, the real attention, o_proj, router || a shared-fc1
stand-in, wait stand-ins whose tails land 2 us before their end, a MoE stand-in that
streams 80 MB), with the engine's window calls: byte compare of every layer's outputs
per arm against off, us per layer per arm and per segment. Built by scripts/build.sh
(not run).
